  "created": "2024-01-07T19:27:37.815Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "It used to be the case that when you used an overlay camera in a world (camera with depth set to higher than that of the default view camera) it would still follow the tracking of your viewport. Now, the camera stays stationary when you rotate your head. Before the way it followed your vr viewport felt intuitive because it wasn't like an image glued to your face. Although I, can imagine the current way could be useful for something like a HUD. Still, it would be great if there were a way to trigger the previous method. Using Udon code to match the rotation of ones head tracking is possible, but yields unsatisfactory results. There is a great deal of jittering that occurs, particularly when you open your menu.\n\nExample world: https://vrchat.com/home/world/wrld_544e7879-d870-42a2-9c7a-99eb95a8e62f\n\nPressing the play button on the monitor at spawn puts you in the game. An overlay camera is looking at a screen game view. Udon code takes the rotation of the head tracking relative to ones playspace tracking and applies that rotation to the overlay camera.",
